35. Search Insert Position

Given a sorted array of distinct integers and a target value, return the index if the target is found. 
If not, return the index where it would be if it were inserted in order.

You must write an algorithm with O(log n) runtime complexity.

Explanation:

start with the prcedure of binary search in arrays.
create a boolean variable called exists and set it to false;
run a for loop to check its existance.if exists set it to true

then create int exists. Run a 2 pointer left right approach to check the array. \
int mid=(left+right)/2;

if mid>target: move right to mid-1 position. 
else if mid<target: move left to mid+1 positon.

now here's the catch- to find out where the element could possibly fit in, we need to check the positon.
Any element usually fits in if there is no space between the left and right - which means right-left<=1.
This <= in it is becuase left and right could possibly overlap each other.
so if it is the case for nums[mid]>target, possbile should be left
if is it nums[mid]<target, possbile should be right+1.


Code:

class Solution {
    public int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {
        int left=0;
        int n=nums.length;
        int right=n-1;
        boolean exists = false;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            if(nums[i]==target){
                exists=true;
                return i;
            }
        }
        
        int possible=0;
        if(!exists){
            while(left<=right){
                int mid=(left+right)/2;

                if(nums[mid]>target){
                    right=mid-1;
                    if(right-left<=1){
                        possible=left;
                    }
                }
                else if(nums[mid]<target){
                    left=mid+1;
                    if(right-left<=1){
                        possible=right+1;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return possible;
    }
}
